{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NAtkrYnAmbvP1rkVnRt98M","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# How to rotate the signing key\n2\t\n3\tRotate the signing key every 90 days, or immediately when you suspect that it leaked.\n4\tThe `auth` service is written in Go and reads the key from the `SIGNING_KEY` secret at\n5\tstartup, so a rotation needs a restart.\n6\t\n7\t## Before you start\n8\t\n9\tYou need `kubectl` access to the `prod` cluster and the `secrets-admin` role.\n10\t\n11\t## Steps\n12\t\n13\t1. Generate the new key:\n14\t\n15\t   ```bash\n16\t   openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out signing.pem\n17\t   ```\n18\t\n19\t2. Add the new key to the secret as `SIGNING_KEY_NEXT`. The `auth` service accepts\n20\t   tokens signed with either key while both are present.\n21\t\n22\t   ```bash\n23\t   kubectl -n auth create secret generic signing --from-file=SIGNING_KEY_NEXT=signing.pem --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -\n24\t   ```\n25\t\n26\t3. Restart the `auth` service:\n27\t\n28\t   ```bash\n29\t   kubectl -n auth rollout restart deployment/auth\n30\t   ```\n31\t\n32\t   Wait until `kubectl -n auth rollout status deployment/auth` prints\n33\t   `successfully rolled out`.\n34\t\n35\t4. Promote the new key: copy the value of `SIGNING_KEY_NEXT` to `SIGNING_KEY` and delete\n36\t   `SIGNING_KEY_NEXT`. Then restart the service again, as in step 3.\n37\t\n38\tTokens that the old key signed stop working after step 4. The `auth` service issues\n39\ttokens that live 15 minutes, so wait 15 minutes between step 3 and step 4.\n40\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec4b6f31-fae0-45c1-9d64-34f07bec5184","uuid":"03deb6bb-f287-4873-986c-ee1db3362698","timestamp":"2026-11-22T20:52:30.615Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/cassiobotaro/Projetos/skills/technical-writing-workspace/iteration-1/eval-6-good-howto-left-alone/without_skill/run-1/outputs/docs/rotate-signing-key.md","content":"# How to rotate the signing key\n\nRotate the signing key every 90 days, or immediately when you suspect that it leaked.\nThe `auth` service is written in Go and reads the key from the `SIGNING_KEY` secret at\nstartup, so a rotation needs a restart.\n\n## Before you start\n\nYou need `kubectl` access to the `prod` cluster and the `secrets-admin` role.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Generate the new key:\n\n   ```bash\n   openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -out signing.pem\n   ```\n\n2. Add the new key to the secret as `SIGNING_KEY_NEXT`. The `auth` service accepts\n   tokens signed with either key while both are present.\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl -n auth create secret generic signing --from-file=SIGNING_KEY_NEXT=signing.pem --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f -\n   ```\n\n3. Restart the `auth` service:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl -n auth rollout restart deployment/auth\n   ```\n\n   Wait until `kubectl -n auth rollout status deployment/auth` prints\n   `successfully rolled out`.\n\n4. Promote the new key: copy the value of `SIGNING_KEY_NEXT` to `SIGNING_KEY` and delete\n   `SIGNING_KEY_NEXT`. Then restart the service again, as in step 3.\n\nTokens that the old key signed stop working after step 4. The `auth` service issues\ntokens that live 15 minutes, so wait 15 minutes between step 3 and step 4.\n","numLines":40,"startLine":1,"totalLines":40}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NAtkrYnAmbvP1rkVnRt98M","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
